<!--BlockML Documentation — a first orientation to BlockML for handbook readers

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Introduction-->
<docs:Introduction xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A first orientation to BlockML for handbook readers
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: what BlockML is, the BML-versus-BOM distinction, and how this handbook is organized
    for a first read, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.Introduction. Body content is
    authored in a03 from that source.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:Introduction" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — BlockML's goals, the BML/BOM split, and the domain-agnostic compiler.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:CoreUseCases" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step, once BML and BOM are distinguished.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Introduction to BlockML</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Introduction</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="What BlockML is">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BlockML is a universal declarative language for describing Blocks —
composable, reusable semantic units that stay independent of how they are later
manifested. One shared grammar describes types, domain Blocks, services, and
documentation alike, and meaning and behaviour are captured explicitly in the
model itself rather than left implicit in prose.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="BML and BOM">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BML is the human- and editor-friendly format used to author a BlockML
model; the BOM is that same model's canonical, immutable, and lossless
representation used for processing. BML stays the authoring surface a person or
tool edits directly, and the BOM preserves everything BML expresses without
silently discarding or reinterpreting it.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="The compiler and renderers">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>The BlockML compiler is domain-agnostic: it parses and validates BML and
BOM without knowing about any particular domain, while domain-specific meaning
and output live entirely in target-specific renderers. This separation keeps the
compiler stable while renderers vary freely across domains and targets.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Reading this handbook">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>This handbook builds outward from a single idea — everything in BlockML
is a Block — through identity and inheritance, the type system, the member
model, and finally the BML authoring format itself, with each page building on
the ones before it.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with the core use cases">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Core Use Cases" summary="See the three problems BlockML is designed to solve.">
                  <target>docs:CoreUseCases</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Introduction>
